{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a major figure in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability and he inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth the regency was held by his mother and later by his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa. Raymond spent much of his early rule under the shadow of powerful nobles and the constant threat of Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. He was known for his cautious nature and his reluctance to engage in risky military campaigns which often put him at odds with more aggressive crusader leaders.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He became regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III and the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. As regent Raymond tried to maintain peace with Saladin through diplomacy and treaties. His policy of negotiation was controversial among other nobles who favored direct confrontation. Raymond also married his cousin Eschiva of Bures to strengthen his claim to the throne and he briefly held the title of Prince of Galilee. His regency was challenged by the rise of the military orders and by powerful barons such as Guy of Lusignan who eventually supplanted him. Raymond was removed from the regency in 1177 after the Battle of Montgisard where the young Baldwin IV took personal command.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nIn the years leading to the fall of Jerusalem Raymond III continued to be a central figure in the defense of the Crusader states. He participated in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 and was captured by Saladin along with many other nobles. He was imprisoned for several months and released after the fall of Jerusalem. Raymond died in 1187 shortly after his release and was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. His legacy is complex as he was seen as both a prudent diplomat and a hesitant leader who missed opportunities to strengthen the Crusader position. His cautious approach contrasted sharply with the more militant policies of his contemporaries and his rule reflects the internal divisions that weakened the Crusader states before their final collapse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 